START = 1420
EN = [
"Archivist Isabel: Elder. I lit the lantern again. It's smaller now, though.",
"Elder Erwin: Yes. I saw. You lit it well. You trimmed the wick a little short. The way I taught you.",
"▷ Read the third line. Aloud.",
"Elder Erwin: …\"When the time comes, let go.\" (His voice cracks.) I am a thousand years late, Master. I could not even find the name. Even so, now, I let go.",
"The Oath of the Order of the Embers · The Original",
"One. Guard the flame.\nTwo. Guard the seals.\nThree. When the time comes, let go.",
"The Eighth King, to His Disciple",
"You write the name. \"Erwin. The name his master gave him. He defied it for a thousand years, and at the last he let go.\"",
"You do not write the name. On that line you write the names of the people he erased. Beginning with Elena.",
"Oscar the Fisher: The nets are heavy. The sea has turned honest.",
"Oscar the Fisher: I think I know now why the Elder could never look at the sea. For fifteen years I couldn't look straight at the north myself. You can't look toward what you're sorry for.",
"Oscar the Fisher: He's laughing. That boy laughed easily.",
"Captain Roran: Head count. …All present. First time in twenty years I've said those words. All present.",
"Captain Roran: The Corps is an expedition now. I'll call on you for the next one too. Come with your bootlaces tied.",
"Archivist Isabel: The scar on my palm. It's stayed in the shape of the letters. Arden. They say it won't fade. That's all right. For an archivist it's a medal.",
"Archivist Isabel: But, Surveyor. There is still one name I haven't written. That name of eight letters. The one even the Elder never found.",
"Archivist Isabel: Letting go and forgetting are different things. I'm going to find it. Without hurrying. I've left one line empty in the Chronicle. Like I did for Helvar.",
"Apprentice Archivist Mia: That day, in the flames, I saw a page that had fallen on the floor. Before it burned.",
"Apprentice Archivist Mia: There were marks written over the letters in violet ink. In reversed writing. I'm left-handed, so I can just read reversed writing.",
"Apprentice Archivist Mia: The original words were 'the eighth stood up.' The words written over them were 'the seven made the eighth stand.' A few strokes apart, and the meaning is the opposite.",
"The Room of Mirrors",
"There is a round room beneath the mansion. Eight mirrors hang in a ring on its wall. The same arrangement as the eight thrones. The mirrors do not show the room. They show something else.",
"The first to the fifth mirrors: a single letter floats in each. The patterns carved on the five stones of the Rune Altar. Patterns you have touched hundreds of times. Here they look like letters.",
"The sixth mirror: the back of a hand. A stroke is burning on it. The stroke that has now gone cold. The seventh mirror: a single round dot. The ink dot you have seen since your first day at the archive.",
"The eighth mirror shows nothing. It trembles in place of showing. Low, long. A sound you have heard somewhere. The cry the dragon gave as it fell in the Void.",
"Archivist Isabel: One lights up each day. Today it's the third mirror. That's what 'today's rune' was. This person has been repeating one letter a day. So as not to forget. For a thousand years.",
"Hidden Story",
"The Chronicle in the Mirror",
"The owner of the violet ink",
"The mist has lifted. All through the house, people of the Ashen Band are just watching us. They aren't stopping us.",
"Below the stairs. A round room. Eight mirrors are hanging there.",
"The portraits on the walls. Every face has been erased. The people of this house don't know their ancestors' faces.",
"I'll look a little longer. I think there's writing on the backs of the mirrors.",
"Drafts in the Scriptorium",
"Paper is stacked from floor to ceiling. You pull out a sheet. A page of the Chronicle. Another sheet. The same page. Every one a copy of the same page. Only one line differs.",
"The original: \"The eighth stood up. He said he would offer up his own name.\" The altered version: \"The seven made the eighth stand. They told him to offer up his name.\" Violet. Reversed writing.",
"A note in the desk drawer: \"That child must not know. Let us give him something to hate. Hatred keeps a person alive. I am the elder disciple. To keep one younger disciple alive, altering one book is nothing.\"",
"The back of the note. Written long afterward. The ink overlaps: \"He lived. He has lived a thousand years. But what is that child doing with that hatred? It is what I gave him. I must take it back. I must gather the pages before he does and burn them.\"",
"The mirror on the desk fogs over. Someone is breathing on the inside. Words appear in the fog, written with a finger. Reversed. \"Have you read it? Then go back. Do not take it to that child.\"",
"Vindicator Doorkeepers of the Head House",
"Belloc, the Scribe in the Mirror",
"The Scribe in the Mirror",
"I told you to go back. You are kind ones. It is the kind ones who break the most. Look at me and you will know.",
"Belloc in the Breaking Mirror",
"I rewrote this story. To keep one child alive.\n…He bore it, you say. That crybaby. Then it is enough. Take the original with you.",
"Archivist Isabel: \"That person stood up. The seven stayed seated. That person said: 'I opposed the dividing. It is right that the one who opposed should pay the price. Only so will those who divided not forget.'\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"That person gave me a mirror. 'Belloc. When the name burns, catch it in this. Keep even a few letters. They are that child's share.'\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"'That child will put his hand in. He will put it in even if he is held back. Then one letter will stay on that child's hand. It will hurt. Tell him I am sorry.'\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"'And when that child becomes able to let me go, give him back the remaining letters. Do not give them before then. With the name in his grasp, that child will try to pull me out. I must not come out.'\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"I saved five letters. That person's last words were these. 'Both of you, write slowly.'\" …Those are the words in the Elder's pen case. The same thing was said to both disciples.",
"▷ So Belloc kept his promise. Waiting for the time to give them back.",
"Archivist Isabel: Yes. And he was afraid. That the time would never come. That's why he made it into the gacha. So someone would touch them every day. So the letters would remain even if he vanished.",
"The Eight Letters Before the Rune Altar",
"Isabel opens the base plate of the altar's stand. Eight sockets. Five hold stones. On three is written \"Not yet.\"",
"You lay paper in the sixth socket. The stroke traced from the back of the Elder's hand. You let one drop of ink fall into the seventh socket. It spreads into a round dot.",
"You lay the echo shard in the eighth socket. The shard trembles. Low, long. The five stones light up in turn. Not one a day, but all together. For the first time.",
"The writing in the three sockets that said \"Not yet.\" has changed. Violet. Reversed. \"Now.\" \"Now.\" \"Now.\"",
"Chronicle of Vestalia · The Empty Line, Filled",
"Orel, King of the Isle of Dawn\nOpposed the dividing, and said it was right that the one who opposed should pay the price.\nOffered up his own name of his own will. Told his two disciples to write slowly.",
"Elder Erwin: Brother Belloc was alive, you say. In the mirror. For a thousand years. …That man always teased me about my handwriting. Said my strokes were hurried. Imitating the Master. 'Slowly, Erwin. Slowly.'",
"Elder Erwin: He altered the Chronicle, you say. To keep me alive. Yes. That is like him. The fool. He knew I could not have borne it. He was right. The boy I was then could not have borne it.",
"Archivist Isabel: Elder. We found the name. All eight letters.",
"Elder Erwin: (He says nothing for a long time.) …Do not say it. No. I do not know. I want to hear it. For a thousand years I wanted nothing else. But if I hear it, I think I will grasp it again. And I have only just let go.",
"Erwin, Talking to Himself",
"Write slowly. Write names clearly.\n…Yes, Master. Now, at last, I write slowly.",
"You tell him the name. Just once, slowly, clearly.",
"You do not tell him. \"It is written in the Chronicle. On the day you are able to let go, read it yourself.\"",
"Haru the Town Clerk: The weather column is filled in by the captain personally. The same words are written in seventeen different hands. 'Suddenly, out of a clear sea.'",
"Haru the Town Clerk: And look at this column. Items picked up on deck after the storm cleared. The captains of eight ships wrote the same thing. 'One scale. The size of a palm. It tingles to the touch.'",
"▷ Was it the same with the ship that brought me?",
"Haru the Town Clerk: Yes. Your ship is the seventeenth. One sailor gave this statement. 'Between the lightning, someone looked down at the deck. At one person after another. And passed on, as if the face they sought was not there.'",
"Haru the Town Clerk: I trust only numbers. Seventeen times out of seventeen is not chance. If you find anything, please let me know as well. I will make a column for it in the ledger.",
"Archivist Isabel: It's a scrap of a banner. The next verse of that song we saw on the watchtower. The first verse was 'We had a king.'",
"Archivist Isabel: \"We had a ship. Even Grandmother never saw its sails. The sister who went out to welcome did not come back. When the storm blows, we look that way.\"",
"▷ The sister who went out to welcome?",
"Archivist Isabel: I don't know. The king's sister, perhaps. The Chronicle hasn't a single line about the eighth king, so there's even less about a sister. And yet these people remembered for a thousand years in a song. Without the names.",
"Archivist Isabel: There's one more note. In crooked writing. 'You said our names aren't in the book. Then write this down. This is all we have.' …I'll write it. All of it.",
"The Wall of the Empty Room",
"The stone wall is covered with words scratched by a fingernail. The same song, many times over. Deep at first, shallower as it goes. The nail wore down.",
"\"We had a ship. Even Grandmother never saw its sails. The sister who went out to welcome did not come back. When the storm blows, we look that way.\"",
"Two knee marks on the floor just inside the door. Left by someone who sat a long time with an ear to the door from outside. The stone beside the marks is slightly melted. Where a hot hand rested.",
"Under the bunk lies something wrapped in cloth. A piece of a broken seal. Lightning with eight forks. On the cloth, in charcoal: \"This is the price for opening the door. Not to that old man. To you.\"",
"Nautilus Shell",
"Oscar the Fisher: The storm stood like a wall. But once through the wall it was calm. The eye, the eye of the storm. Clear enough to see the moon.",
"Oscar the Fisher: There were ships there. Not like my boat, big ships. With lightning painted on the sails. I counted. The lightning had eight forks. Not seven.",
"Oscar the Fisher: People stood on the decks. All looking one way. At my boat. Ready to throw a line. Like people about to haul someone aboard when they come.",
"▷ And then what happened?",
"Oscar the Fisher: They saw my face and lowered the lines. Not with disappointed faces. Just… faces that said 'not this one, next.' Faces that had done it a thousand times. And then the storm pushed my boat back out. Without harming a single sail.",
"Oscar the Fisher: Every night I light a lantern on the ice. For my son to come. Those people are doing the very same thing. For a thousand years or so. With a storm in place of a lantern.",
"Archivist Isabel: The seal first. '…Who Awaits.' On the back of the silver coin, in the same lettering, 'wages for the day of going out to welcome.' The broken part is 'The One.' 'The One Who Awaits.' It was the admiral's title.",
"Archivist Isabel: The score is 'The Song of Going to Welcome.' Only the place where a name goes is empty. It wasn't erased. It could never be written. Because the name they meant to call had burned.",
"Archivist Isabel: The scrap of the log. \"Who is coming has been forgotten, yet the order is not to stand down from the welcome.\" And there was a scale at every seal. The storm has been to the side of every guardian. To see if that person was there.",
"▷ Why hide inside a storm?",
"Archivist Isabel: It's the same as the people in the mirror. And the clerks in the boxes. If you are somewhere the world cannot see, the forgetting cannot reach you. The eye of a storm is the only such place on the sea.",
"Archivist Isabel: Do you remember that line in the logbook? The route the grey cloak sold to Captain Cornelia. 'Where the eighth eye is.' It wasn't the eye of a seal. It was the eye of the storm. Where the eighth king's sister is.",
"Archivist Isabel: …The grey cloak sent the captain's ship into that storm. Why? To see what happens when the storm looks into a ship? To learn what that sister remembers?",
"▷ It wasn't the captain who froze the sea.",
"Archivist Isabel: Kael's last words. …Yes. It wasn't the captain. And it wasn't the storm. It was the person who sent that ship there.",
"A Voice Heard From the Eye of the Storm",
"A ship is coming.\n…Make ready to receive it. This time it may be that person.",
"What Was Left in the Cabin",
"Ropes hang on the wall of the rotted cabin. So decayed they crumble at a touch. And yet the coils have kept their shape. Rescue lines, coiled to pay out in a single cast. Dozens of coils.",
"A crew roll is nailed to the table. Name, duty, day boarded, day discharged. The 'day discharged' column is empty from the very top to the very bottom.",
"Scribbles in the margin of the roll, in many hands: \"Not today either.\" \"Nor today.\" \"The Admiral smiled. Said, 'They will come tomorrow.'\" \"Asked the Admiral who is coming. After a long while she said, 'I will know when they come.'\"",
"At the very bottom, in letters blurred by water: \"We want to go ashore. But we cannot go ashore and leave the Admiral alone. She waits without even knowing whom she waits for.\"",
"Oscar the Fisher: …I at least knew who I was waiting for. Because I knew the name. A thousand years without even the name. That means not even knowing how to put the lantern out.",
"A shipwreck. I don't know water. But if it's the business of those who wait, I'll look at it myself.",
"The cabin wall. Ropes hanging there. Look at how they're coiled.",
"They rotted still ready to go out and rescue. …Go on up first. A wet leg obeys even less.",
"The Palace on the Sea",
"The Empty Banquet Hall",
"A long table is laid in the middle of the palace. Plates, cups, spoons. There is no dust. The table was laid today. There is no food. She has forgotten what the one who is coming likes, and so could not serve it.",
"The two seats at the head stand side by side. The chairs are identical. The seats of twins. One chair is worn from a thousand years of sitting; the other is as straight as new.",
"On the back of the unworn chair is a place where something was once carved. It is smooth. Where a name was. Beneath it, a line gone over countless times with a fingernail: \"Your seat.\"",
"At the end of the table sits a model ship, frozen. It does not belong with the rest. It is about fifteen years old. On its stand, in a scribe's neat hand: \"When this ship comes, please call the name.\" Signed with the drawing of a gloved hand.",
"Drowned Beneath the Storm",
"Scylla, the Storm Empress",
"Guardians Beneath the Storm",
"The One Who Went to Welcome",
"The Storm Empress, who for a thousand years has come out to welcome someone",
"The Storm Empress, Falling Asleep",
"You are not that person.\n…And yet yours is the face of one who knows where that person is.",
"Archivist Isabel: One of the sailors told me. The Admiral smiled at the end. For the first time in a thousand years she said something other than 'they will come tomorrow.'",
"Archivist Isabel: What she said, that sailor couldn't hear either. Because of the thunder. Only you will have heard it, Surveyor.",
"Chronicle of Vestalia · The Name That Went to Welcome",
"The One Who Awaits, Admiral of the Isle of Dawn\nForgot whom she had come out to welcome, and still laid the table for a thousand years\nDay discharged: (left empty. She has gone to welcome him.)",
"You said: \"He is in the Void. It was the road he chose himself. He did not forget you. It was everyone who forgot him.\"",
"You said nothing, and set Oscar's lantern down before her.",
]
